---
description: Never surface technical error detail in the UI; route it to dev logs instead
alwaysApply: true
---

# User-Facing Errors

Anything rendered on screen must be plain, descriptive prose that tells the
person what happened and what to do next. Never render an error code, HTTP or
SQLSTATE status, exception class name, raw JSON body, stack trace, or a server
message you have not deliberately written for users.

Technical detail is not discarded; it goes to `lib/src/core/dev_log.dart`
(`logFail`), which is compiled out of release builds.

## Rules

- Convert every caught error with `toAppError` / `errorMessage` from
  `lib/src/core/app_error.dart`. Never build display text from an exception.
- Put machine-readable values in `AppError.code` (for branching) and raw detail
  in `AppError.technical` (for logs). Only `AppError.message` reaches the UI.
- Only pass server text through when it was written for users: our Postgres
  functions raise friendly prose and set `hint`, and `manage-staff` returns a
  friendly `error` field. Everything else collapses to a generic message.
- Log every action with `logAction` / `logDone` / `logFail` so failures are
  diagnosable without showing internals to the user.

```dart
// ❌ BAD: leaks a JSON body, a code, and a class name onto the screen
setState(() => _error = error.toString());
return AppError('Verification failed: $error');
return AppError(authException.message, code: authException.code);

// ✅ GOOD: friendly text on screen, detail in the log
try {
  await repository.clockIn(...);
} catch (error, stack) {
  logFail('clock_in', error, stack);          // full detail, debug only
  setState(() => _error = errorMessage(error)); // safe prose
}

// ✅ GOOD: unrecognised cause stays vague on screen
return AppError(
  'Something went wrong on our end. Please try again, and contact your '
  'administrator if it keeps happening.',
  code: 'auth_error',
  technical: '${error.statusCode} ${error.message}',
);
```
